    <!-- The Artificial Neuron -->
    <section class="section" id="artificial-neuron">
      <span class="section-label">Foundation Concepts</span>
      <h2>The Artificial Neuron</h2>
      <p>An artificial neuron is a simple computational unit inspired by biological neurons. It takes multiple inputs, applies weights to each, sums them, adds a bias term, and applies a nonlinear activation function. Despite this simplicity, networks of billions of neurons can learn remarkably complex patterns.</p>

      <p>Mathematically, a neuron with inputs x1, x2, ... xn computes: output = activation(w1*x1 + w2*x2 + ... + wn*xn + b), where w are weights and b is bias.</p>

      <p>The weights determine how important each input is. The bias shifts the activation threshold. The activation function introduces nonlinearity, without which deep networks would be no more expressive than single-layer linear models.</p>

