    <!-- Forward Propagation -->
    <section class="section" id="forward-propagation">
      <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
      <h2>Forward Propagation: Computing Network Output</h2>
      <p>Forward propagation is the process of computing the network output given an input. Data flows from input layer through hidden layers to the output layer. Each layer applies its transformation: multiply by weights, add bias, apply activation.</p>

      <p>For a network with 3 layers: input → hidden1 → hidden2 → output, forward propagation computes a1 = f(W1*x + b1), then a2 = f(W2*a1 + b2), then output = f(W3*a2 + b3). This composition of functions enables representation of complex nonlinear patterns.</p>

      <h3>Implementing Forward Propagation</h3>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">relu</span>(<span class="va">x</span>):
    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">maximum</span>(<span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="va">x</span>)

<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">sigmoid</span>(<span class="va">x</span>):
    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="nm">1</span> <span class="op">/</span> (<span class="nm">1</span> <span class="op">+</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">exp</span>(<span class="op">-</span><span class="va">x</span>))

<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">forward_propagation</span>(<span class="va">x</span>, <span class="va">weights</span>, <span class="va">biases</span>):
    <span class="st">"""Forward pass through network with 3 layers"""</span>
    <span class="cm"># Layer 1: input -> hidden1</span>
    <span class="va">z1</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">dot</span>(<span class="va">x</span>, <span class="va">weights</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>]) <span class="op">+</span> <span class="va">biases</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>]
    <span class="va">a1</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">relu</span>(<span class="va">z1</span>)  <span class="cm"># Hidden layer activation</span>

    <span class="cm"># Layer 2: hidden1 -> hidden2</span>
    <span class="va">z2</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">dot</span>(<span class="va">a1</span>, <span class="va">weights</span>[<span class="nm">1</span>]) <span class="op">+</span> <span class="va">biases</span>[<span class="nm">1</span>]
    <span class="va">a2</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">relu</span>(<span class="va">z2</span>)  <span class="cm"># Hidden layer activation</span>

    <span class="cm"># Layer 3: hidden2 -> output</span>
    <span class="va">z3</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">dot</span>(<span class="va">a2</span>, <span class="va">weights</span>[<span class="nm">2</span>]) <span class="op">+</span> <span class="va">biases</span>[<span class="nm">2</span>]
    <span class="va">output</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">sigmoid</span>(<span class="va">z3</span>)  <span class="cm"># Output layer (binary classification)</span>

    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="va">output</span>, {<span class="st">'z1'</span>: <span class="va">z1</span>, <span class="st">'a1'</span>: <span class="va">a1</span>, <span class="st">'z2'</span>: <span class="va">z2</span>, <span class="st">'a2'</span>: <span class="va">a2</span>, <span class="st">'z3'</span>: <span class="va">z3</span>}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- Backpropagation Concept -->
    <section class="section" id="backpropagation">
      <span class="section-label">Advanced Concepts</span>
      <h2>Backpropagation: Learning Through Error</h2>
      <p>Backpropagation is how neural networks learn. It computes gradients of the loss function with respect to all weights and biases, enabling gradient descent to update them toward lower loss.</p>

      <p>The algorithm works in reverse: compute loss, propagate the error backward through layers, and accumulate gradients. Each neuron learns how much it contributed to the overall error, and adjusts its weights accordingly.</p>

      <p>Mathematically, backpropagation applies the chain rule from calculus: dL/dw = dL/doutput × doutput/dhidden × dhidden/dw. For deep networks with many layers, this chain multiplication is computed efficiently through dynamic programming.</p>

      <div class="callout warning">
        <strong>Vanishing gradients:</strong> In very deep networks, gradients can become exponentially small as they propagate backward, making learning slow or impossible. This is one reason ReLU is preferred over sigmoid/tanh: it doesn't "squash" gradients as much.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Universal Function Approximation -->
    <section class="section" id="universal-approximation">
      <span class="section-label">Theory</span>
      <h2>Neural Networks as Universal Function Approximators</h2>
      <p>A key theoretical result: a neural network with just one hidden layer containing enough neurons can approximate any continuous function arbitrarily well. This is why neural networks are so powerful—they can theoretically learn any pattern given sufficient capacity and data.</p>

      <p>In practice, deep networks often learn more efficiently than shallow networks because they can build representations hierarchically. A vision model might learn edge detectors in early layers, then shapes in middle layers, then objects in deep layers. This compositional structure matches how we conceptually break down complex problems.</p>

      <p>However, with great power comes great responsibility: networks with enough capacity to memorize the training data (overfitting) must be carefully regularized and evaluated.</p>
    </section>

